const meanings=[
 'Recognize AH as IPsec integrity and data-origin authentication without confidentiality; retain contextual cryptographic/protocol support with precise numbered scope withheld.',
 'Recognize DKIM as verification of covered canonicalized message content under a signing domain, not automatic visible-author authentication or a safety verdict.',
 'Recognize DMARC as Author Domain alignment using a passing SPF or DKIM result, with requested handling/reporting policy rather than mandatory universal receiver action.',
 'Recognize DNSSEC as origin authentication and integrity for signed DNS data through validated trust, not encrypted DNS transport; precise numbered implementation scope withheld.',
 'Recognize IPsec suite context only; hold the all-encrypted and combined-AH-and-ESP reading pending explicit configuration and optional-service qualifications.',
 'Recognize MITM/on-path as adversary positioning to intercept or alter endpoint communications; narrow attack vocabulary, not a demonstrated cryptographic break.',
 'Recognize NGFW as a network firewall capability category combining application-aware inspection and prevention features under actual product and deployment conditions.',
 'Recognize NIPS as network intrusion-prevention capability in an inline enforcement path that can block detected activity, not a guarantee that every attack is prevented.'
];
const qualifications=[
 'HOLD authentication as unique-person identity or safe plaintext. AH excludes mutable IP-header fields and does not encrypt; anti-replay effectiveness requires receiver checks and sound security-association/key handling. Header changes such as NAT complicate deployment. AH is not ESP, tunnel/transport mode, an authenticated-encryption construction or proof of IPsec configuration competence. Preserve prior AH-card context-only scope; no packet, replay window, peer or VPN was configured or tested.',
 'HOLD message origin as visible From alignment, human authorship or trusted content. Verification binds signing domain d= to covered selected headers and canonicalized body, potentially limited by l=; it does not promise all-byte immutability, encryption or safe attachments. Key retrieval/trust, algorithms and custody matter. DMARC alignment is a separate check. Preserve the existing DKIM support identity lacking 4.5 as a metadata mismatch, not an approved schema edit; no DNS lookup or signature was verified.',
 'HOLD coordinates SPF and DKIM as requiring both to pass: one passing aligned identifier is sufficient. SPF envelope/HELO, DKIM signing domain and visible Author Domain are distinct identities with strict/relaxed alignment rules. Requested reject/quarantine/report handling remains subject to receiver policy; pass is not encryption, guaranteed delivery, trustworthy content or human identity. Use current RFC9989 rather than superseded RFC7489; no DNS policy, mail flow, receiver decision or report was tested.',
 'HOLD validate responses as universal protection from poisoning or all DNS threats. Validation needs appropriate trust anchors, chain, signatures and time/algorithm handling; unsigned/insecure, bogus and indeterminate states must remain distinct. Signed origin does not imply safe destinations or availability. DNSSEC supplies no query confidentiality and is not DoH/DoT, CAA issuance authorization or filtering policy. Existing support identity has empty codes; do not invent a precise 1.4/2.4/4.5 assessment or edit it. No resolver or DNS answer was validated.',
 'HOLD authenticated and encrypted using AH and ESP as automatic suite-wide encryption or mandatory simultaneous protocols. AH has no confidentiality; ESP services depend on selected transforms and configuration, including integrity-only use. Security-association policy, peer/key establishment, modes and enabled receiver checks determine protection; not all traffic is automatically protected. Preserve prior AH and the inspected lesson optional-encryption premises. No precise support link or full 1.4/3.2 implementation assessment is approved; no VPN was configured.',
 'HOLD intercepts or alters as ability to read/forge any correctly authenticated encrypted channel. Attack viability depends on path/relay position, endpoint identity verification, key/protocol and user/device assumptions. Passive observation, replay, endpoint compromise and forward secrecy are separate concerns. Correct peer authentication and integrity constrain impersonation/modification; encryption alone without identity binding is insufficient. No attack, decrypted capture or prevention was demonstrated. Metadata-only MITM references are not rendered tooltip exposure.',
 'HOLD feature bundle as uniform enabled product, guaranteed detection/prevention, WAF/CASB/SIEM/HSM equivalence or automatic TLS payload visibility. Actual rules, inspection parsers, signatures, licenses, capacity, placement and TLS decryption/endpoint visibility govern coverage; evasion and false positives/negatives remain. Encrypted traffic may expose metadata while hiding content. TLS inspection requires an appropriate authorized termination/decryption path and trusted endpoint configuration, not simply an NGFW label. No appliance or traffic was inspected.',
 'HOLD can block as all malicious traffic blocked or label proving active enforcement. Inline placement, enabled prevention/rules, visibility, tuning, performance and failure behavior matter; passive observation alone does not block packets in the path. False positives can block benign traffic and false negatives can miss attacks; encrypted payloads need an appropriate observation/decryption point. NIPS is not HIPS, NIDS, generic IPS, HSM or the combined HIPS/NIPS card identity. No sensor, rule or throughput was tested.'
];
